{"body": "## What the expression says\n\nThe expression is a worked-out application of the frozen non-Lean semantic class **Rival-Story Semantics (RSS)** to a body of warehouse prose that has been withheld. It does not restate RSS abstractly; it shows RSS operating on concrete disagreements: duplicate authority, supersession across revisions, expiry versus supersession, negative availability, tie-breaking across bookings, replica-time relativity, and revision gaps. Along the way it reconstructs a per-booking procedure (group by revision, keep the greatest, treat tied greatest-revision holds as unresolved alternatives rather than sums), and a global procedure (sum per-booking reservations under a reading, subtract from capacity, admit the possibility of a negative result).\n\nIts main substantive moves are these:\n\n1. It reads the snapshot as a **viewpoint**, not a ledger. Capacity and logical time are coordinates of that viewpoint; the records arrive as a bag; the \"snapshot\" is one viewpoint among many.\n2. It separates **exact duplication** (inert in all readings) from **near-duplication** (candidate for conflict, not covered by the duplicate-authority statement).\n3. It makes **supersession** structural within a reading and within a booking, and explicitly conjectural across readings.\n4. It treats **expiry** and **release** as reading-dependent effects, not as facts, and gives two coherent readings of each that produce opposite booking reservations.\n5. It treats **negative availability** as a signal about over-reservation, not an arithmetic error, and prefers the reservation set over the sign.\n6. It treats **inter-booking compatibility** as invisible unless a viewpoint includes a resource model.\n7. It treats **criticism** as targeting a specific element (record, rule, history, snapshot, coordinate, assumption), and **accounts** as never merged.\n8. It treats **open questions** as carried forward by inquiry states, never resolved by RSS itself.\n9. It closes with an explicit list of what RSS obstructs and cannot express, including the operational cost of retained uncertainty and the absence of a mechanism for aligning viewpoints across replicas.\n\nThe prose-escape block at the end asserts that no Lean fragment is being obstructed. That claim is itself a commitment of the expression, and it is worth noting that the selected carrier's own statement (\"Non-Lean Semantic Class … not a proof assistant language … deliberately noncomputable\") is consistent with it, but that consistency does not establish that the source contained no Lean. The escape says only that the expression is not quoting or obstructing Lean content, not that the source lacked it.\n\n## What it leaves unresolved\n\nSeveral things remain up in the air despite the breadth of the expression:\n\n- **Whether the bag is union, intersection, or single-replica view.** The expression says RSS \"does not decide,\" and that this is represented as a viewpoint-relative matter. But it also says the snapshot is \"described as replicated,\" without deciding which replication semantics the frozen source actually intends. This is left as the tenth disagreement, not as a settled reading.\n- **Whether supersession is structural across readings.** RSS's stated relation says this is \"a matter of conjecture.\" The expression repeats the conjecture without settling it.\n- **Whether the \"tilt\" in the source is a commitment or a hedge.** The prose-escape block explicitly says the source's phrase \"tilts toward the first\" is carried as the source's own hedge, not upgraded. That is an interpretive choice, and it could be contested.\n- **Whether revision numbers are contiguous, and whether gaps are evidence of loss.** Declared an account-level assumption, not a fact.\n- **What expiry *does* to supersession.** Two readings are given; the source is said not to resolve them; RSS is said not to choose. The conjecture \"supersession is structural and expiry is evaluative\" is marked as a conjecture about a reading, not as the semantics' own position.\n- **What a release means across revisions.** Reading-dependent; not resolved.\n- **Whether any single-number availability is authorized.** The expression says a projection is \"a loss of information\" and that different projections can disagree even when they respect the snapshot. Whether that is a defect or a feature is left to accounts.\n- **What the physical resources are.** Marked invisible unless a resource model is added.\n- **How to compare accounts.** RSS \"lacks a common metric,\" per the obstruction list, and the expression does not supply one.\n- **Whether the closing source sentence (\"Keeping these disagreements visible is not a failure … only honest form\") is normative, descriptive, or rhetorical.** The expression calls it \"the source's own normative claim,\" but does not adjudicate it.\n\n## Assumptions it uses\n\nThe expression relies on several assumptions that are not themselves derived from RSS's primitives:\n\n- **That the frozen source is coherent enough to host rival readings at specific points** (expiry, release, tie-breaking, replication). The expression does not argue for this; it assumes that locating rival readings is a charitable reading.\n- **That \"authority\" is the right word for what exact duplicates fail to add.** \"Authority does not accumulate by repetition\" is a gloss on \"duplicate deliveries convey no additional authority,\" but the meaning of \"authority\" in the source is not defined in the expression.\n- **That \"greatest revision present\" is the natural grouping.** The expression says \"the natural grouping,\" which is a claim about salience, not a theorem.\n- **That per-booking reconstruction is prior to global availability.** This is asserted, not derived.\n- **That a set or interval of availability is the honest output under unresolved ties.** The expression says \"honest,\" which is a normative word; RSS's own primitive inventory says projections are losses of information, not that non-projection is honest.\n- **That criticisms and refutations are local to accounts and do not force global agreement.** This is stated in the RSS primitives and repeated, but its suitability for the frozen source is not argued.\n- **That no Lean fragment is present in the selected carrier's handling of the source.** The prose-escape asserts this; it is not established by the expression itself.\n\n## What could criticize it\n\nSeveral criticisms are available:\n\n**Internal to RSS.** The semantics is noncomputable and refuses to select a reading. The expression celebrates this as honesty, but the criticism it lists (\"pushing the problem downstream, where it may be resolved badly or inconsistently\") is left unadjudicated. A reader could say the expression performs the very deferral it describes, and that without a metric for comparing accounts it cannot distinguish a substantive account from a pseudo-account. The expression acknowledges \"it lacks a common metric\" but does not treat this as a defect of RSS—only as an obstruction to comparison.\n\n**Against the semantics' fit to the source.** The expression claims the source \"tilts toward\" one reading of expiry but does not quote that tilt or examine its wording. If the frozen source in fact settles the expiry question, the expression's insistence that both readings remain live would be a misrepresentation. The prose-escape block's hedge (\"the source's own hedge, not upgraded to a commitment\") is itself a contestable reading of a phrase we are not shown.\n\n**Against the vocabulary.** \"Story,\" \"bag,\" \"coordinate,\" \"viewpoint,\" \"conflict,\" \"liveness,\" \"reading,\" \"projection,\" \"account,\" \"inquiry state,\" \"conjecture,\" \"commitment,\" \"refutation\" are all technical terms introduced without definitions external to RSS. A critic could say the vocabulary is a net that catches everything and distinguishes too little: any disagreement becomes a \"reading,\" any unresolved question becomes an \"open question,\" any rejection becomes a \"refutation.\" The expression even notes this risk (\"too permissive, too vague, or too detached from operational needs\") but does not rebut it.\n\n**Against the non-Lean stance.** The prose-escape asserts no Lean fragment is obstructed. But non-Lean semantics is not automatically freer than a Lean translation; it is differently constrained. It cannot state a theorem, cannot check a proof, and cannot mechanically distinguish a refutation from a contradiction-in-prose. A critic could say this is not a virtue but a limit, and that the expression's framing of Lean as something merely to *not be* is itself a projection onto a contrast the source may not make.\n\n**Against the reconstruction's pessimism.** The expression says \"the reconstruction's only honest form\" is to keep disagreements visible—quoting a source sentence and endorsing it in passing. A critic could say this is a rhetorical move: by calling visibility the only honest form, the expression immunizes itself against demands for resolution. That is exactly the criticism the expression lists but does not answer.\n\n**Against the treatment of negativity.** \"Availability can be negative\" becomes \"the sign is a signal, not an error; the set of reservations is more informative than the sign.\" But if capacity is meant as a hard constraint, negative availability indicates a constraint violation, not merely a signal. The expression concedes this criticism (\"the word 'capacity' may be misleading\") but treats it as a criticism of the primitive, not of the reconstruction.\n\n**Against the order of exposition.** The expression begins with a first-person declaration (\"I will not compute one answer\") that sits uneasily with RSS's own framing, which is about accounts, not voices. If the \"I\" is an account, it is not named; if it is the semantics speaking, the semantics has no first person. This is a small stylistic matter, but it blurs the line between the semantics and an instance of it.\n\n## Competing readings of the expression\n\nThere are at least two defensible readings of what the expression is doing:\n\n**Reading A (exegetical).** The expression is an exposition of RSS applied to a specific source, intended to show that RSS can host the source's own rival readings without dissolving them. On this reading, the expression succeeds insofar as it locates primitives and relations in the source and reports conflicts; its failures are gaps in coverage (resource models, replica protocols, provenance).\n\n**Reading B (performative).** The expression is not merely describing RSS; it is enacting it. The first-person \"I will not compute one answer,\" the repeated \"RSS carries it forward,\" and the closing refusal to adjudicate are part of the semantics' rhetorical register. On this reading, the expression is a demonstration that a non-Lean, noncomputable semantics can be *used*, not just *described*. Its risks are correspondingly larger: if it reads as a manifesto, it may be criticized for the very permissiveness it admits, and if it reads as an apology, it may be criticized for smuggling normative claims (\"the reconstruction's only honest form\") into what presents itself as neutral representation.\n\nThese readings are not incompatible, but they pull the expression in different directions: A asks whether the source was accurately partitioned; B asks whether the enactment is coherent on its own terms.\n\n## Source quotations visible in the expression\n\nThe expression quotes or near-quotes several fragments of the withheld source:\n\n- \"duplicate deliveries convey no additional authority\"\n- \"A higher revision supersedes lower revisions\"\n- \"Availability can be negative\"\n- \"People may disagree about whether uncertainty should be retained\"\n- \"the snapshot description tilts toward the first\"\n- \"Keeping these disagreements visible is not a failure of the reconstruction; it is the reconstruction's only honest form\"\n\nEach of these is presented as a fragment of the frozen prose. None is given with surrounding context, so the interpretation of each fragment is not checkable against the source from within the expression itself. The prose-escape block's claim that no Lean fragment is being obstructed cannot be verified from the expression alone, and it should not be treated as a proof that the source contained no Lean—only as a commitment of the expression not to represent one.\n\nThe reconstruction's strength depends on whether these fragments are representative of the source, and that is a question the expression cannot answer about itself.", "commitments": "## What the expression says\n\nThe expression is a worked-out application of the frozen non-Lean semantic class **Rival-Story Semantics (RSS)** to a body of warehouse prose that has been withheld. It does not restate RSS abstractly; it shows RSS operating on concrete disagreements: duplicate authority, supersession across revisions, expiry versus supersession, negative availability, tie-breaking across bookings, replica-time relativity, and revision gaps. Along the way it reconstructs a per-booking procedure (group by revision, keep the greatest, treat tied greatest-revision holds as unresolved alternatives rather than sums), and a global procedure (sum per-booking reservations under a reading, subtract from capacity, admit the possibility of a negative result).\n\nIts main substantive moves are these:\n\n1. It reads the snapshot as a **viewpoint**, not a ledger. Capacity and logical time are coordinates of that viewpoint; the records arrive as a bag; the \"snapshot\" is one viewpoint among many.\n2. It separates **exact duplication** (inert in all readings) from **near-duplication** (candidate for conflict, not covered by the duplicate-authority statement).\n3. It makes **supersession** structural within a reading and within a booking, and explicitly conjectural across readings.\n4. It trea